\begin{abstract}
Counterfeit currency remains a major challenge for financial systems worldwide and poses a significant threat to the economic stability of countries such as Bangladesh. Traditional counterfeit detection methods rely on manual inspection or specialized hardware, which can be time-consuming and costly. Recent advances in computer vision and deep learning provide promising solutions for automated currency authentication. This paper presents a deep learning-based approach for detecting counterfeit Bangladeshi banknotes using Vision Transformers (ViT), specifically the Data-efficient Image Transformer (DeiT) architecture. The proposed system utilizes a pretrained DeiT-Tiny model that is fine-tuned on a dataset of Bangladeshi banknote images collected from two publicly available sources: the JaalTaka benchmark dataset and the Bangladeshi Taka Note dataset from Kaggle. The combined dataset contains 10,412 images of genuine and counterfeit banknotes. All images are preprocessed and resized to match the model input requirements before training. The model is trained to classify banknotes into two categories: genuine and counterfeit. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves a classification accuracy of 97.88\% on the test dataset. Additional evaluation metrics including precision, recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC further confirm the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed method. The results indicate that transformer-based architectures are capable of capturing subtle visual features of Bangladeshi banknotes and can provide an efficient and scalable solution for automated counterfeit detection. The proposed system has the potential to assist financial institutions, banks, and retail sectors in improving currency verification processes and reducing the circulation of counterfeit currency.


\end{abstract}

