\section{Introduction}

Counterfeit currency represents a persistent challenge to global economic stability and financial integrity. According to international law enforcement agencies, billions of dollars in counterfeit notes circulate worldwide each year, undermining public confidence in monetary systems and causing significant economic losses \cite{1}. Bangladesh is also affected by the circulation of forged banknotes, which can disrupt financial transactions and create economic instability \cite{2}. Traditional counterfeit detection methods rely on manual inspection or specialized hardware devices such as ultraviolet scanners, watermark detectors, and magnetic sensors \cite{3}. Although these techniques can be effective, they are often time-consuming, require trained personnel, and may still be prone to human error when dealing with sophisticated counterfeit notes that closely replicate security features.

Recent advances in deep learning have significantly improved the capabilities of automated visual inspection systems \cite{4} \cite{5}. Deep learning models have demonstrated remarkable success in computer vision tasks such as object detection, image classification, and pattern recognition. In particular, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) have dominated image classification research for more than a decade, achieving high performance across a wide range of applications \cite{6}. CNN-based approaches have also been applied to currency authentication problems, where they automatically learn discriminative features such as texture patterns, color distributions, and structural characteristics of banknotes \cite{7}.

Despite their success, CNN architectures primarily focus on extracting local spatial features through convolutional operations. Recent developments in transformer-based architectures have introduced a new paradigm for visual recognition tasks. Vision Transformers (ViT), first proposed by Dosovitskiy et al. \cite{8}, adapt the transformer architecture originally designed for natural language processing to image classification. Instead of processing images through convolutional layers, ViT divides an image into fixed-size patches that are linearly embedded and processed as a sequence through transformer encoder layers using self-attention mechanisms. This architecture enables the model to capture long-range dependencies and global contextual relationships across the entire image.

The Data-efficient Image Transformer (DeiT) further extends the Vision Transformer framework by introducing optimized training strategies and knowledge distillation techniques that significantly reduce the data requirements for training transformer models \cite{9}. As a result, DeiT models can achieve competitive performance with smaller datasets while maintaining high computational efficiency. These characteristics make DeiT particularly suitable for specialized applications such as counterfeit currency detection where large-scale datasets may not always be available.

In this study, we propose a transformer-based deep learning framework for detecting counterfeit Bangladeshi banknotes using the DeiT-Tiny architecture. The dataset used in this research is constructed by combining two publicly available datasets: the \textit{JaalTaka: A Benchmark Dataset for Detecting Counterfeit Bangladeshi Banknotes} available on Mendeley Data and the \textit{Bangladeshi Taka Note} dataset available on Kaggle. The combined dataset contains a total of 10,314 images consisting of both genuine and counterfeit banknotes. These images were organized into training , validation and testing subsets and preprocessed by resizing them to $224 \times 224$ pixels to match the input requirements of the Vision Transformer architecture.

The proposed model is trained to classify banknote images into two categories: genuine and counterfeit. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that the proposed approach achieves a classification accuracy of 97.88\% on the test dataset. In addition to accuracy, several performance metrics including precision, recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC are used to evaluate the robustness of the model. Furthermore, presentation attack detection metrics such as Attack Presentation Classification Error Rate (APCER) and Bona Fide Presentation Classification Error Rate (BPCER) are also analyzed to assess the system’s effectiveness in detecting counterfeit notes.

The main contributions of this work can be summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item Development of an automated counterfeit Bangladeshi banknote detection system using the Data-efficient Image Transformer (DeiT-Tiny) architecture.
\item Construction of a combined dataset containing 10,314 banknote images collected from the JaalTaka benchmark dataset and the Bangladeshi Taka Note dataset.
\item Implementation of mixed-precision training to improve computational efficiency during model training.
\item Comprehensive evaluation using multiple metrics including accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, APCER, and BPCER.
\item Detailed analysis of the model’s classification performance for detecting genuine and counterfeit Bangladeshi banknotes.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II reviews related work in counterfeit currency detection and transformer-based computer vision models. Section III describes the proposed methodology and model architecture. Section IV presents the experimental results. Finally, Section V concludes the paper and discusses potential future research directions.
\section{Related Work}

Research on counterfeit Bangladeshi banknote detection has evolved significantly over the past decade, progressing from traditional image processing techniques to advanced deep learning models\cite{10}\cite{17}. Early approaches primarily relied on handcrafted feature extraction methods to identify security elements embedded in banknotes, such as micro-printing, watermarks, holograms, and optically variable inks. Classical computer vision algorithms including Optical Character Recognition (OCR), Scale-Invariant Feature Transform (SIFT), and Support Vector Machines (SVM) were widely used to analyze these features\cite{11}. Although these methods achieved moderate accuracy levels of approximately 93\%, their performance was often sensitive to variations in illumination, image quality, and background conditions.

With the rapid advancement of deep learning, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) became the dominant approach for image-based counterfeit detection. CNN-based architectures are capable of automatically learning hierarchical feature representations from raw images, eliminating the need for manual feature engineering. Several studies applied CNN architectures such as AlexNet and VGG-based models to banknote classification tasks. For example, modified AlexNet architectures combined with Multi-SVM classifiers have demonstrated improved detection performance on Bangladeshi currency datasets\cite{12}. Despite these improvements, CNN-based approaches still face challenges in complex real-world scenarios where images may contain variations in lighting, orientation, occlusion, and noise.

More recently, transformer-based models have emerged as powerful alternatives to CNN architectures in computer vision tasks. Vision Transformers (ViT) leverage self-attention mechanisms to model global relationships between image regions, enabling the network to capture contextual dependencies across the entire image. This capability allows transformer-based models to better handle variations in scale, orientation, and background compared to traditional convolutional networks. Recent research applying ViT models to Bangladeshi currency recognition tasks has reported significant improvements in classification performance, achieving accuracy levels as high as 99.93\% on enhanced datasets containing new denominations such as the 200 Taka note and images captured under diverse environmental conditions\cite{13}\cite{14}.

In addition to improved classification performance, ViT-based systems have demonstrated robustness in handling challenging real-world conditions, including variations in illumination, rotation, and noise. These characteristics make transformer-based models highly suitable for practical applications such as automated currency authentication systems and mobile-based banknote recognition tools designed to assist visually impaired users. Vision Transformer-based systems have been successfully applied to Bangladeshi coin recognition, achieving up to 99.39\% accuracy \cite{15}.

Although research specifically focused on counterfeit detection of Bangladeshi banknotes using Vision Transformers remains relatively limited, the strong performance of transformer architectures in related currency recognition tasks suggests significant potential for their application in counterfeit detection frameworks\cite{16}. Motivated by these advancements, this work investigates the application of the Data-efficient Image Transformer (DeiT) architecture for automated detection of counterfeit Bangladeshi banknotes using a combined dataset collected from multiple publicly available sources.

\section{Methodology}

This section describes the dataset preparation, preprocessing pipeline, model architecture, and training configuration used for counterfeit Bangladeshi banknote detection.

\subsection{Dataset}

The dataset used in this study consists of images of genuine and counterfeit Bangladeshi banknotes collected from two publicly available sources: the \textit{JaalTaka: A Benchmark Dataset for Detecting Counterfeit Bangladeshi Banknotes} available on Mendeley Data and the \textit{Bangladeshi Taka Note} dataset available on Kaggle. After combining and organizing the datasets, the final dataset contains a total of 10,412 banknote images\cite{18}.

The dataset follows a standard \textit{ImageFolder} directory structure with two classes:

\begin{itemize}
\item Class 0: Counterfeit 
\item Class 1: Genuine 
\end{itemize}

To train and evaluate the model effectively, the dataset was divided into three subsets: training, validation, and testing. The split ratio used in this study is approximately 80\% for training, 10\% for validation, and 10\% for testing. The training set is used to learn model parameters, the validation set is used for hyperparameter tuning and monitoring model performance during training, and the test set is used for final performance evaluation.
