All images are resized to $224 \times 224$ pixels to match the input requirements of the DeiT architecture while preserving important visual features such as texture patterns and security elements present in Bangladeshi banknotes.
\subsection{Data Preprocessing and Augmentation}

Data preprocessing is applied to ensure consistent input formatting and improve the model's ability to generalize to unseen samples. Different transformation pipelines are used for training and evaluation phases.

\textbf{Training transformations:}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Image resizing to $224 \times 224$ pixels
\item Random horizontal flip with probability $p = 0.5$
\item Conversion to tensor format
\item Normalization using mean $[0.5, 0.5, 0.5]$ and standard deviation $[0.5, 0.5, 0.5]$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Validation and testing transformations:}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Image resizing to $224 \times 224$ pixels
\item Conversion to tensor format
\item Normalization using the same parameters as the training set
\end{enumerate}

The horizontal flip augmentation increases the diversity of the training data and enables the model to learn orientation-invariant visual features. Normalizing pixel values to the $[-1,1]$ range improves numerical stability during training and aligns the input distribution with the pretrained DeiT model.

\subsection{Model Architecture}

The proposed system, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:methodology}, employs the Data-efficient Image Transformer (DeiT) Tiny architecture, specifically the \textit{deit\_tiny\_patch16\_224} model.This architecture provides an effective balance between computational efficiency and classification performance.

The model processes input images through several components:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Patch Embedding Layer:} The $224 \times 224$ input image is divided into non-overlapping patches of size $16 \times 16$, resulting in $14 \times 14$ (196) patches. Each patch is linearly projected into an embedding space of dimension 192.

\item \textbf{Positional Encoding:} Learnable positional embeddings are added to the patch embeddings to preserve spatial relationships between image patches.

\item \textbf{Transformer Encoder:} The embedded patch sequence is processed through 12 transformer encoder layers. Each layer consists of multi-head self-attention with 3 attention heads and a feed-forward multilayer perceptron (MLP) with an expansion ratio of 4.

\item \textbf{Classification Head:} The final transformer output corresponding to the class token is passed through a fully connected layer that outputs probabilities for two classes: genuine and counterfeit banknotes.
\end{itemize}

