\subsection{Training Configuration}

The model is trained for 10 epochs using the following training configuration:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Loss Function:} Cross-Entropy Loss for binary classification
\item \textbf{Optimizer:} AdamW optimizer with learning rate $3 \times 10^{-4}$
\item \textbf{Optimizer Parameters:} $\beta_1 = 0.9$, $\beta_2 = 0.999$
\item \textbf{Batch Size:} 32
\item \textbf{Data Loading:} 2 worker threads with pinned memory for efficient GPU data transfer
\end{itemize}

Automatic Mixed Precision (AMP) is used through \textit{torch.cuda.amp.GradScaler} to accelerate training and reduce GPU memory consumption. Mixed precision training allows computations to be performed using both 16-bit and 32-bit floating-point formats, resulting in faster training while maintaining numerical stability.

The AdamW optimizer is selected due to its decoupled weight decay regularization, which improves generalization compared to the standard Adam optimizer.

All experiments are conducted on a CUDA-enabled GPU environment. To ensure experimental reproducibility, a fixed random seed (42) is applied across NumPy, PyTorch CPU, and CUDA operations.
\section{Experimental Results}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

To evaluate the performance of the proposed model, several standard classification metrics are used:

\begin{itemize}
\item Accuracy
\item Precision
\item Recall
\item F1-score
\item ROC-AUC
\end{itemize}

These metrics provide a comprehensive assessment of classification performance.

\subsection{Classification Performance}

The experimental results indicate that the proposed transformer-based model performs effectively in detecting counterfeit banknotes. The model achieved a test accuracy of 97.88\%, demonstrating its strong capability in distinguishing genuine and counterfeit currency. A summary of the evaluation metrics is presented in Table~\ref{tab:performance}.

