The high classification accuracy suggests that Vision Transformer-based architectures can effectively learn discriminative representations for counterfeit banknote detection. Despite the relatively lightweight DeiT-Tiny architecture, the model successfully captures both fine-grained texture patterns and broader structural characteristics present in banknotes. 

Furthermore, the model achieved a BPCER of 0.00\%, indicating that all genuine banknotes in the test set were correctly classified. This is particularly important for practical financial applications, where misclassifying genuine currency can lead to unnecessary operational issues. In addition, the model demonstrates strong counterfeit detection capability, achieving a detection rate of 93.87\%, which corresponds to an APCER of 6.13\%. These results highlight the effectiveness of the proposed approach in maintaining reliable verification of genuine notes while accurately identifying counterfeit currency.
