\section{Discussion}

The proposed Vision Transformer achieved 97.88\% test accuracy, demonstrating its effectiveness in learning discriminative features for counterfeit banknote detection. Despite using the lightweight DeiT-tiny architecture, the model successfully captures both local texture irregularities and global structural inconsistencies through the self-attention mechanism. The zero BPCER (0.00\%) indicates perfect recognition of genuine banknotes, which is crucial in real banking operations to avoid customer inconvenience and processing delays. Additionally, the 93.87\% counterfeit detection rate (APCER = 6.13\%) reflects a strong balance between security and usability.

Compared to traditional approaches, the proposed method outperforms CNN-based models and hand-crafted feature-based methods, highlighting the advantage of transformer-based long-range dependency modeling and end-to-end feature learning. Furthermore, transfer learning using ImageNet-pretrained weights significantly improved convergence speed and reduced the need for large labeled datasets.

However, several limitations remain. Model performance may vary across different currencies, imaging conditions, and physically worn or damaged banknotes. In addition, robustness against adversarial attacks and the lack of interpretability require further investigation. Future work will focus on multi-currency dataset expansion, attention visualization for explainability, adversarial training, and evaluation under diverse real-world conditions.

\section{Conclusion}

This paper presents a successful application of Vision Transformers for Bangladeshi banknote counterfeit detection, achieving 97.88\% test accuracy using the lightweight DeiT-tiny architecture. The proposed model demonstrates several important advantages over traditional approaches. It achieves higher accuracy than previously reported CNN-based methods while maintaining strong computational efficiency. The model also provides balanced performance, with 0.00\% BPCER (perfect genuine banknote acceptance) and 6.13\% APCER (low counterfeit acceptance), which is highly desirable for real-world financial security applications.

Mixed-precision training further improves computational efficiency, enabling real-time inference on moderate hardware suitable for deployment in bank teller stations, ATM kiosks, and retail point-of-sale systems across Bangladesh. The end-to-end learning capability of the Vision Transformer eliminates the need for manual feature engineering by automatically learning discriminative representations from Bangladeshi banknote images.

Moreover,the high ROC-AUC score of 99.65\%indicates strong discriminative power across different classification thresholds, allowing system operators to adjust decision thresholds based on operational requirements such as higher security or faster transaction processing. The self-attention mechanism is particularly effective in capturing both local texture irregularities (e.g., printing artifacts, micro-pattern distortions) and global structural inconsistencies (e.g., misalignment of security features) that are characteristic of counterfeit Bangladeshi currency.

These findings establish Vision Transformers as a promising alternative to CNN-based solutions for financial document authentication in Bangladesh. The combination of high accuracy, low false rejection of genuine notes, and efficient computation makes the proposed approach suitable for practical deployment in banking and retail environments.

Future work will focus on extending the framework to multi-denomination Bangladeshi banknotes, incorporating additional imaging modalities such as infrared and ultraviolet scanning for enhanced security feature analysis, and developing explainable AI techniques to improve model transparency for forensic and regulatory use. Furthermore, federated learning strategies may enable collaborative model training across financial institutions in Bangladesh while ensuring data privacy and security.

